==Latin==

===Etymology===
{{PIE root|la|gʷeyh₃}}
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*gʷīwō}}, from {{inh|la|ine-pro|*gʷíh₃weti||to live}}. The ''x'' and ''c'' in {{m|la|vīxī}} and {{m|la|vīctum}} were introduced by analogy with other verbs.

Cognate with Old English {{m|ang|cwic||alive}} (English [[quick]]), Old Church Slavonic {{m|cu|sc=Cyrl|жити|tr=žiti}}, Ancient Greek {{m|grc|sc=polytonic|βίος}}, Sanskrit {{m|sa|sc=Deva|जीवति|tr=jīvati}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|Classical}} {{IPA|/ˈwiː.woː/|lang=la}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|vīvō|vīvere|vīxī|vīctum|conj=3}}

# I [[live]]
#* {{Q|la|Cicero|Catiline|Oratio in Catilinam Prima in Senatu Habita|2|quote=O tempora, o mores! Senatus haec intellegit, consul videt; hic tamen '''vivit'''. '''Vivit'''?|trans=Shame on the age and on its principles! The senate is aware of these things; the consul sees them; and yet this man '''lives'''. '''Lives'''!}}
#* {{Q|la|Cicero|Catiline|Oratio in Catilinam Prima in Senatu Habita|1|quote=Quam diu quisquam erit qui te defendere audeat, '''vives''', et '''vives''' ita ut nunc '''vivis''', multis meis et firmis praesidiis obsessus ne commovere te contra rem publicam possis. Multorum te etiam oculi et aures non sentientem, sicut adhuc fecerunt, speculabuntur atque custodient.|trans=As long as one person exists who can dare to defend you, you '''shall live'''; you '''shall live''' as you do now, surrounded by my many and trustworthy guards, so that you shall not be able to stir one finger against the republic: many eyes and ears shall still observe and watch you, as they have hitherto done, though you shall not perceive them.}}
# I am [[alive]], I [[survive]]
# I [[reside]] in
#* {{Q|la|Cicero|Catiline|Oratio in Catilinam Prima in Senatu Habita|9|quote=O di immortales, ubinam gentium sumus? Quam rem publicam habemus? In qua urbe '''vivimus'''?|trans=O ye immortal gods, where on earth are we? What is the government we have? In what city are we '''living'''?}}

